  "description": "Stars never promote a movie past one with more yes-votes",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 1 / Stars: 'Because stars are consulted only after yes_votes, a star can never promote a movie past one with more yes-votes; it separates only films the approval count has already tied. Where yes-vote counts differ, stars are irrelevant.'",
  "rationale": "N_FINALISTS is 2, 5 attendees. m1: 5 rows (u1-u4 yes, u5 no) -> coverage 1.00, yes 4, approval 0.80, stars 0. m2: 5 rows (u1-u3 yes, u4-u5 no) -> coverage 1.00, yes 3, approval 0.60, stars 0. m3: 4 rows (u1 yes STARRED, u2 yes STARRED, u3 no, u4 no) -> coverage 0.80, yes 2, approval 0.50, star_votes 2. All three are eligible and clear APPROVAL_FLOOR. Ranking by yes-votes: m1 = 4, m2 = 3, m3 = 2. There is no tie anywhere, so no rung of the chain is consulted and finalist_boundary_tiebreak is null: m1 and m2 are promoted and m3 is excluded, despite holding the only two stars in the group. A star is a tie-breaker, never a score: an implementation that added stars to the ranking key (2 + 2 = 4), or ordered star_votes above yes_votes, would promote m3 over m2. Runoff over {m1, m2}: m1 preferred 3 (u1, u2, u3) to 2 (u4, u5) -> Condorcet winner.",
